# Compilation targets

All backends consume **IR EML** (never the source AST directly).

| Target | Role |
|---|---|
| **Stack `.eml`** | Post-order `ONE` / `EML` textual dump with UTC header (`eml compile -of eml`) |
| **Stack `.beml`** | Packed-bit binary of the same stream (`eml compile -of beml`, default) |
| **EML tree text** | Nested `eml(...)` and `1` (`.teml` input / IR tree dumps; not a compile `-of` yet) |
| **Native binary** | Lower IR EML through **LLVM** to a machine-code executable |
| **.NET CLI** | Emit a **.NET CLI DLL/executable**; **may use** .NET math |
| **Java bytecode** | Emit a **Java class**; **may use** Java math |
| **Interpreter** | Evaluate IR EML in-process (same executable) |

Native LLVM lowering should realize `eml(x, y) = exp(x) - ln(y)` (complex). .NET and Java backends may call the host language’s `exp` / `log` (and other math) instead of expanding everything to a single opcode.

`eml compile --output-format` / `-of` currently supports `eml` (textual stack IR) and `beml` (binary stack IR). `--format` / `-f` is removed. Future `-of` values: `cli` → .NET DLL, `bytecode` → Java, `binary` → LLVM native. Same-format compile (`eml`→`eml`, `beml`→`beml`) is rejected. See `eml-cli`.

